    "source" : "2503.04950_pg23_images/imageFile115.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 130,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 409.188, 230.508, 425.028, 230.988 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "2503.04950_pg23_images/imageFile116.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "caption",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Caption",
    "id" : 131,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 154.47, 540.059, 224.202 ],
    "linked content id" : 130,
    "font" : "SFRM1000",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We have to check that the map is well-deﬁned and surjective. We claim that the ﬁrst µ1 +1 entries of the bottom row of any σ′ ∈ M(n+1) are 0. Suppose for contradiction that there was a non-zero value a among these ﬁrst µ1+1 cells. Then there are at least n+1−|µ|−(µ1 +1) = n−|µ|−µ1 entries right of a. Of these, at most |η| − 1 are allowed to be non-zero (since a is one of the |η| non-zero values in the labeling). Hence a would contribute at least n − |µ| − µ1 − (|η| − 1) > i inversion triples formed by taking a, the phantom cell below it, and a 0 entry to the right of it. This contradicts that inv(σ′) = i for σ′ ∈ M(n+1)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 132,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 82.719, 540.038, 141.791 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1000",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "This implies that if we let σ be obtained from a σ′ ∈ M(n+1) by deleting the bottom left cell and shifting the bottom row to the left by one, then σ ∈ M(n) and γn(σ) = σ′, which implies that γn is well-deﬁned and bijective. Indeed, the ﬁrst µ1 cells of the bottom row of σ will be labeled 0 and so the status of descents and inversion triples doesn’t change when going from σ′ to σ. Thus, σ′ and σ have the same inv and maj values."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 133,
    "page number" : 1,
